00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.190
That's interesting because I was talking

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.410
about disc compression, but the vertebrae,

00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.560
which are the actual bony
bodies in between the discs,

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:23.790
can fracture as well.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:25.560
Must have been a lotta
damage to his spine,

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.316
but definitely can recover
with surgery from that.

00:09:28.316 --> 00:09:29.406
(air whooshing)
- Whoa!

00:09:29.406 --> 00:09:30.420
Oh, look out. We got trouble.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:31.253
This is gonna hurt.

00:09:31.253 --> 00:09:32.970
(flames hissing)
- Oh, oh, oh, oh, my God!

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:35.953
For the people in the stands!
(cars zooming)

00:09:35.953 --> 00:09:39.030
(tires squealing)
And for the car. Oh, my God.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:43.020
Oh, my God, multiple hits.
(vehicles scraping)

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:44.490
The whole race is over.

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:47.220
- [Announcer] So Jeffrey
is alert, at least,

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.650
and conscious and able to move.

00:09:49.650 --> 00:09:50.490
- Oh, my God.
- O don't know about you guys,

00:09:50.490 --> 00:09:52.230
but I'm breathing a little
bit better right now.

00:09:52.230 --> 00:09:53.550
- Well, me too.
- I mean, I'm not

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.470
because you're still not out of danger

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:56.820
until you're in the hospital.

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:58.470
Even in the hospital,
when we have a patient

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.450
in one area, and we need to transfer them,

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:03.660
whether it's from a regular
inpatient floor unit

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.290
to the ICU or from the
ER to the patient room.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.600
That transportation can only happen

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.240
when the patient's stable,
and things go wrong

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.400
during that transportation
that you need to be present

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.220
and ready to activate a
code blue, a rapid response,

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:18.997
and treat the patient
right then and there.

00:10:18.997 --> 00:10:21.330
"He suffered a concussion,
facial laceration,

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.857
multiple bruises, right wrist fracture,

00:10:22.857 --> 00:10:25.050
and a small fracture to a vertebra, and is

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.600
in intensive care unit in
serious but stable condition."

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.290
Wow, I would say that it's
probably a very severe concussion

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:34.560
when you're being tossed
around at that rate of speed.

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.510
I mean, that's incredible.
(air whooshing)

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:39.067
- Just as I walk outta
infirmary, they said,

00:10:39.067 --> 00:10:42.063
"Lee Petty just went over the
turn three and four wall."

00:10:42.063 --> 00:10:44.460
(tires squealing)
(vehicles crashing)

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.110
- Oh!
(glass shattering)

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:47.820
This looks like something from the scene

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.735
of a car game called Carmageddon.

00:10:49.735 --> 00:10:51.480
(laughs) I don't know if
you guys remember that game,

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:53.490
or Twisted Metal.
- Twisted Metal for sure.

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:55.770
- Yeah, I think I also
couldn't afford the real game,

00:10:55.770 --> 00:10:57.588
but I got my hands on a demo disk.

00:10:57.588 --> 00:10:59.070
- Oh, okay.
- And I just played the demo

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:00.660
over and over. (laughs)
(signal beeping)

00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:01.770
- Went in to talk to him.

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:04.110
He was about half conscious and all,

00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:05.910
just tore all to pieces,

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:07.430
cut up everywhere.
- Oh, my God.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:09.900
- Ribs broke, and punctured lung.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.090
Just his leg all, just all busted,

00:11:12.090 --> 00:11:13.097
all just hanging there.
- Oh, my God.

00:11:13.097 --> 00:11:15.750
That's the biggest danger that
I always tell you guys about,

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:16.830
whenever there's a rib fracture,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:19.740
about puncturing your lung
and developing a pneumothorax.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.560
Also, less talked about
but equally dangerous is if

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:26.160
that rib fractures in an area
where it nicks your aorta.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.780
You have massive internal bleeding.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:29.257
That's a deadly, deadly injury.

00:11:29.257 --> 00:11:31.110
"Lee Petty's car was
destroyed, and then they took

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.450
his broken, apparently
lifeless body to the hospital.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:35.700
It was some time before he
could have broken bones fixed.

00:11:35.700 --> 00:11:37.350
They waited to see if he would stabilize

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:38.280
as he lay in a coma.

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:40.050
He spent four months in the hospital.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.450
Petty did return to racing,
but he was never quite

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:44.040
as competitive again," wow.
(air whooshing)

00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:46.440
Oh, throwback. Okay,
let's see what happened.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.630
Oh, oh, wow!
(mouse clicking)

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.910
Okay, those are the
ones I'm talking about,

00:11:50.910 --> 00:11:52.410
where you're going high rate of speed,

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:53.880
and then you come to an instant stop.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:55.380
That's where you lose your life.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:56.940
- [Announcer] There is
Michael Waltrip now,

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:58.710
standing up, climbing out from around

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:00.930
that shredded, mangled
mass of sheet metal.

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:01.850
- What, he's just walking out?
- That was

00:12:01.850 --> 00:12:04.590
a Pontiac Grand Prix
just a few moments ago.

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:06.150
- Yeah, Ben, I think I'm all right.

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.670
Got some contusions and a
little bit of confusion,

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.680
but that's probably not too unusual.

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:13.140
- That's not unusual? That is unusual.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.780
When we decide whether or
not to scan someone's head

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:20.070
for an intracranial bleed,
it's based off of certain signs

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:22.590
and symptoms, but also
the mechanism of injury,

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:25.500
and going 100 miles an hour
or whatever that was to zero

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.110
is most definitely a
high-speed accident warranting

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:29.520
a full-body CAT scan.
(mouse clicking)

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:31.290
- [Waltrip] I think I'm all
right, and we'll go home

00:12:31.290 --> 00:12:32.520
and get a good night's rest tonight.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:33.900
- No, you don't go home!

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.400
You go straight to the hospital.

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:36.233
I hope he went to the hospital.

00:12:36.233 --> 00:12:37.066
(mouse clicking)
- Try to figure out something.

00:12:37.066 --> 00:12:40.500
- You know, my seat stayed
secured in the frame rail,

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.870
and the only frame rail,
really, that stayed

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.070
as a part of the back of the car.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.830
This part came and went that.
- Wow, I think the only thing

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:48.990
that really saved him here
was that he hit the outside

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:50.230
of his car, not his own side.

00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:52.740
If it was the driver's
side that had that impact,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.034
it would've been a very different outcome.

00:12:54.034 --> 00:12:55.830
(air whooshing)
- Alonso, let's go!

00:12:55.830 --> 00:12:58.681
This is McLaren days, not Ferrari days.

00:12:58.681 --> 00:13:01.800
(vehicle crashing)
Oh, there goes his front tire.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:03.550
Oh, it's so hard to make
out what just happened.

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:05.850
- Is he okay?
- The whole axle

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:08.880
looked like it went.
(cars zooming)

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:11.043
He walked away like that? That's him?

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.455
Wait, is that Esteban Gutierrez?

00:13:14.455 --> 00:13:15.570
(bubble popping)

00:13:15.570 --> 00:13:16.710
That's my friend!

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:18.330
Look at all these pictures of me and him.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:20.223
- You see the sky. Then
you see the ground.

00:13:20.223 --> 00:13:22.470
You see the sky, and you know
you exactly where you are,

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:24.900
and you keep moving,
moving, you want to stop.

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:25.890
Then they kinda stop.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:27.300
I saw a little space throughout,

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:28.590
and I said, "I go out quickly."

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:31.020
And my mom will watch TV, so I want

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:32.400
to be out quite quickly.
- Wow.

00:13:32.400 --> 00:13:33.307
- And I'll say that I'm okay.

00:13:33.307 --> 00:13:35.280
"I feel fine physically, but
everything hurts a little

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:37.110
because you move around
so much at those speeds.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:38.280
The knee is what bothers me most

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:39.420
as it hit the steering column,

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:41.790
but I feel very lucky," very lucky.

00:13:41.790 --> 00:13:43.680
Everything is gonna hurt for a while,

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:45.690
and honestly, you should
be under observation

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.600
because if you are even having
a small bleed internally,

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.460
you wanna make sure that
you're able to act quickly.

00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:53.580
So I have a funny story
with Esteban Gutierrez.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:54.990
We went to a family dinner.

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:58.830
I took him in my Audi TTS,
and I was gonna drop him off

00:13:58.830 --> 00:14:01.110
at his place and then go home,

00:14:01.110 --> 00:14:03.750
and I'm with a Formula 1 driver.

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:06.480
I'm gonna drive and try and look cool,

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:09.540
and because of that, I turned
in to the Holland Tunnel

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:11.670
in the city, and I didn't mean to do that.

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.770
So I was like, "Oh, my God, I
turned into the wrong thing."

00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:15.240
So I was like, "Let me
just back up real quick

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:16.590
'cause I'm not that far in,"

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:18.540
and as soon as I started backing up,

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:20.220
you see a cop walk out onto the street

00:14:20.220 --> 00:14:21.720
and go, "Excuse me, sir.

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.240
Come on. Come on," and had me pull up.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:26.106
Checked my windows for tints.

00:14:26.106 --> 00:14:27.990
(bubble popping)
Started writing me tickets.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.940
I was like, "Officer, I'm so sorry.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:31.110
I made this mistake.

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.130
I'm a physician.

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:33.690
This is a Formula 1 Driver.

00:14:33.690 --> 00:14:35.100
He's like, "I couldn't care less.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:38.580
Here's your 10 ticket,"
but yeah, that goes to say

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:41.010
that even you have a Formula 1 driver

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:43.110
in your passenger seat,
don't try and show off

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:44.880
because things will go wrong.
(static hissing)

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.020
- [Announcer] But the
rest of the field is-

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:47.310
- Oh, no. Oh, no.

00:14:47.310 --> 00:14:48.683
This is gonna be the worst one, isn't it?

00:14:48.683 --> 00:14:53.683
(cars zooming)
(tires squealing)

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:55.890
(laughs) This looks like a skit.

00:14:55.890 --> 00:14:59.100
The only thing that I'll say
is this is pretty low speed,

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:00.630
so I guess that's a little bit safer,

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:02.460
but these vehicles weren't as safe,

00:15:02.460 --> 00:15:04.140
so that's not necessarily true.

00:15:04.140 --> 00:15:05.430
You know what's really scary?

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:07.740
When you watch some of those videos

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.140
when there is a blizzard,
and there's low visibility,

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.570
and then there's an accident
and another accident,

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:14.850
and all of a sudden,
there's cars just sliding

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.980
into other cars, and then people come out

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:18.720
to see what's going on, and
they're getting trapped.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:20.970
Never exit your vehicle if you're

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:22.110
in one of those situations.

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:23.820
Call for help. Stay in your vehicle.

00:15:23.820 --> 00:15:26.100
Because you don't know
what's coming behind you.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.500
That's the scary part, and
if you do exit, run away.

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:30.210
Don't stay on the highway.
(air whooshing)

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.400
I mentioned earlier that I flew an F-16.

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:33.960
Check out that video right here.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:36.390
I'm doing barrel rolls,
upside down twisties,

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:38.010
9 G's, over 9 G's.

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.360
Seriously, click here to watch that,

